orm a chow. But I don't have W3. W6 is already in the meld. So maybe not. So perhaps the W tiles are not helpful for more chows.\n\nLooking at the B5, since there are two left, and I have one, maybe keeping it is better. If I discard B5, someone else can take it, but if I need it for a pung, I might miss it. So probably better to keep B5.\n\nSo among the valid actions, the safer tiles to discard would be T2, T6, T9, W4, W1, etc. Considering that T2 has only one left, maybe discard T2. But if I discard T2, it might be taken by someone else. Alternatively, T9 is a terminal, and if I discard it, maybe someone needs it for their hand. But since I don't have a pair or chow in T9, maybe it's better to discard T9.\n\nWait, looking at the unshown T9: 1 left. If I discard T9, there's only one left. So someone might take it. But if I discard T2, which is part of a possible chow, but since there's only one left, it's also likely to be taken. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should discard a tile that's part of a less needed suit. For example, my hand has W, T, B, and J. The meld is in W. If I can focus on another suit, maybe T or B. But I have only B5. So maybe not helpful.\n\nAnother approach: look for which tile, when discarded, gives me the most flexibility to form a winning hand. For example, if I discard T2, I can keep T5 and T6, which could form a chow if I get T4 or T7. If I discard T9, I still have T5 and T6. So maybe either is okay. But since T2 has only one left, maybe it's better to discard T2 to avoid someone else getting it and using it against me. But I'm not sure.\n\nWait, the game history of the next player includes Chi B2, which might indicate they have B1 and B3. The player across has Chi W2, so they might have W1 and W3. The previous player has Chi B4, so they might have B3 and B5. So maybe some players are building in certain suits.\n\nIf I discard T2, and the next player has T1 and T3, they can Chi it. But if I discard T9, maybe someone has T8 and T7 to form