The unshown J2 has two left. So the chance is possible.\\n\\nBut if they play J2 now, they can't form a pair with it. However, if they keep it, maybe they can use it for a pair. But without a pair, they can't win. So maybe it's better to keep J2 in case they can form a pair.\\n\\nAlternatively, if they play T6, they still have T5. If they can get a T4, they can form a chow. But T4 has only one left. If they play T6, they lose that possibility.\\n\\nHmm. This is tricky. Let me think again.\\n\\nThe user's melds are two chows in the Characters suit. Their hand has W1, W4, W8, W9, T1, T5, T6, J2. To form a winning hand, they need two more melds and a pair. Let's see if they can form any.\\n\\nLooking at the Dots suit (T1, T5, T6). T1 is a single, T5 and T6 are singles. No chow possible. The Bams (no B tiles in their hand). The Characters: they have W1, W4, W8, W9. No chows possible here. J2 is a single.\\n\\nSo they need to form two more melds and a pair. The only way is to form a concealed pung or a chow with the tiles they have or draw.\\n\\nBut since they can't form any now, they need to wait. So the best strategy is to discard a tile that is least likely to be needed by others and that doesn't block their potential to form a winning hand.\\n\\nLooking at the valid actions, the best options are probably J2, T1, or W1. Between these, J2 is a Dragon tile, which is more likely to be needed for pungs. T1 and W1 are terminals, which are less likely to be needed for chows once the melds are formed.\\n\\nThe player across has a meld of T1-T2-T3, so T1 is already part of their chow. Playing T1 might be safe. Similarly, the player across has a W1-W2-W3 chow, so W1 is already part of their chow. So playing W1 might be safe.\\n\\nBetween T1 and W1, which is better? The unshown T1 has 2 left, W1 has 3. So W1 has more left, making it safer. So playing W1 might be better.\\n\\nBut why is W1 in the valid actions? Because the user has one W1. If they play it, they discard it. Consi